#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172890} :activity/id #uuid "b43f46e4-16a6-5109-897b-7d61b6455c74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/id #uuid "a45cb440-1b11-5eea-ac7d-09ef4b31794b" :topic/math-academy-id 3335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171831 :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519598}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "a2aa8190-261c-5faa-a399-c992ada54b4e" :step/math-academy-id 30552 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555870}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "af3ae02e-e0d1-5714-a823-36990c3416dc" :step/math-academy-id 30553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171833 :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541095}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "136b060b-1871-5958-8dc0-d7310998c040" :step/math-academy-id 30578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173154 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP.\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n*A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix \n\n![](images/b3/b3970eb885720ce2aa4394918c6c5f9f343e26aa60039df219abf56398f096db.svg)\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{T}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A.$ Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "c729ab04-2eef-5b7c-a28e-3d0f55428c54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "337f89b5-9c83-5bda-ac19-7d80b551f82e" :step/math-academy-id 31262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172882}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172166}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519655} :activity/id #uuid "64fc3270-931c-55b0-ab11-941f63cb1739" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/id #uuid "a45cb440-1b11-5eea-ac7d-09ef4b31794b" :topic/math-academy-id 3335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c224e271-277d-5f1a-9cc2-c5f3c1736629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & -\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404621} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e4a6c1-e440-5739-9e5b-4073535092ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404622} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae4b95-ac89-5527-8195-692cf68f4405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404623} {:answer/id #uuid "5537d4ab-5df2-5c89-8196-ce1e4ae511b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a3f76-d0f1-5a60-8575-903fd51dffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac5900c-9744-5a1f-9961-900e2ff573d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46cbbf2d-cd23-5aab-9cf1-375dd44b0200" :question/math-academy-id "q-94046" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae94915d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d49197c-8ea0-5037-8658-b8800ba11d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394882} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb14de4-81a2-5a22-9b22-a9c795e5d80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394883} {:answer/id #uuid "2d287e51-ff4c-5d64-aaf3-306e594af48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} {:answer/id #uuid "298cf117-73a5-5dba-8a49-4651e4dc4bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394885} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a2c500-01a4-5141-91a3-878c1f2e4f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1f9db6-ad26-5de3-b141-6cab0e8d74e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc9e75e4-89e6-5c5c-b897-fce34f0588db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94026" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f94d183-1b9a-5126-80a9-56929d485a13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca90f25-8a2a-5154-8abc-8f1caaac1737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355048} {:answer/id #uuid "4e874988-4b8c-5650-b515-a02bdcf8db24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355364} {:answer/id #uuid "5180d0c3-657e-5b75-ac2b-25066a4ea962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355390} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb170a0-8ac1-5736-bef7-d6a15eeabe0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356341} {:answer/id #uuid "d73f5496-3e39-5d24-8f7b-c48a9b96fdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356341} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda65562-ed2d-5933-b1e5-85c35db9bffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b031f1a5-d4c2-57ba-adf6-a3fa295e4440" :question/math-academy-id "q-94054" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6dd7af92-0a90-5fa3-955d-021bfada8c3b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519042} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} {:answer/id #uuid "90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519230} {:answer/id #uuid "de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519359} {:answer/id #uuid "e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} :answer-field/id #uuid "567b4465-6b20-53a1-be8a-d9caef043ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22c685a9-35f7-5e7f-8909-4579d1ac4e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-93598" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9f77e54a-ce06-5ad1-86c7-0ccf9bdad4ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518993} {:answer/id #uuid "5c76486d-0cd9-5267-b109-d5ac0d8c7703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519130} {:answer/id #uuid "6b14d34e-024d-5c98-a7cd-041c6930d3b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519149} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c17d5d-2d75-57cc-95a7-828178d428fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519254} {:answer/id #uuid "e90c6a79-88fe-5546-8423-a7c541bc4746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518993} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94003" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."} :step/id #uuid "c59605b5-04a9-57e2-b8a6-b29858b31bee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519624}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518979}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555891} :activity/id #uuid "8204430f-7743-5e1b-97bd-65f00f3fe35a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/id #uuid "a45cb440-1b11-5eea-ac7d-09ef4b31794b" :topic/math-academy-id 3335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491564} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491601} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491728} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491740} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/math-academy-id "q-94049" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} {:answer/id #uuid "87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555316} {:answer/id #uuid "88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555318} {:answer/id #uuid "a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555395} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf95aab-26ad-5d4c-96ff-37b37822372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7e02b11-9226-5c5b-9fda-bf26ea5fa1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-93602" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1383bfc2-4360-5709-a0a8-f10b27226553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354836} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf62f53-4cca-53fd-9904-3006d9e5e4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c84ca9d-2a09-535e-ac82-9783439e70e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355622} {:answer/id #uuid "8f61224e-9396-5066-9c8e-bd0d738416d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c07977-b2ba-5f8d-bb10-ef441705d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} :answer-field/id #uuid "110fa06e-fdc4-596b-8460-688e417bf995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fd6de92-b207-5bcd-bc32-b23f271f69c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94039" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555398} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555463} {:answer/id #uuid "d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555507} {:answer/id #uuid "f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555612} {:answer/id #uuid "fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627} :answer-field/id #uuid "6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94051" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491507} {:answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} {:answer/id #uuid "6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491646} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491649} {:answer/id #uuid "7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4fd8bc-59fc-526b-b28d-cb3ea2256c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6397dc6a-e670-5a27-9059-a00e14d9ffee" :question/math-academy-id "q-94024" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555904}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554995}]}
